1
00:00:00,700 --> 00:00:05,880
Today, we will be discussing the spatial join.

2
00:00:07,277 --> 00:00:15,377
In GIS, a join occurs when rows from two layers
are matched by location instead of a shared key.

3
00:00:15,715 --> 00:00:28,708
Here each point picks up the name of the district
it falls inside. That is a spatial join.

4
00:00:30,117 --> 00:00:37,767
Traditionally, this is done by testing each point
against each polygon, one pair at a time.

5
00:00:38,067 --> 00:00:47,367
The test draws a ray from the point and counts the
edges it crosses. An odd count means inside.

6
00:00:48,976 --> 00:01:02,476
That works for a handful of points. Now a real case:
a million Uber pickups and 263 New York taxi zones.

7
00:01:03,867 --> 00:01:12,107
Start with one pickup. We want to know
which taxi zone it is in.

8
00:01:12,587 --> 00:01:23,227
With no index, the computer has to ask every zone,
one at a time. Is this pickup inside you?

9
00:01:23,587 --> 00:01:35,307
It keeps asking after the first yes, because zones can
overlap. Even the far side of the city gets a test.

10
00:01:35,667 --> 00:01:44,507
That is 263 tests to place one pickup.
Only one of them says yes.

11
00:01:45,107 --> 00:01:58,027
Now repeat that for every one of the 1,028,136 pickups.
A million pickups times 263 zones is 270 million tests.

12
00:01:58,747 --> 00:02:12,587
Only about one million of those tests say yes.
The other 269 million were wasted work.

13
00:02:14,223 --> 00:02:24,423
An R-tree draws the smallest rectangle around each
zone, then groups nearby rectangles into bigger ones.

14
00:02:24,833 --> 00:02:38,902
Checking a rectangle is cheap. A pickup only gets the
full test for boxes it lands in: about 2, not 263.

15
00:02:41,089 --> 00:02:52,789
Grid indexes turn the problem around. Lay cells
over the map and stamp every pickup with a cell ID.

16
00:02:53,331 --> 00:03:04,760
A cell that sits fully inside one zone is settled
by matching integers. No geometry at all.

17
00:03:05,302 --> 00:03:17,260
Only pickups in cells that cross a boundary
still need a point-in-zone test.

18
00:03:18,833 --> 00:03:30,533
The same few blocks of Manhattan under four grid systems.
Each one tiles the map with a different shape.

19
00:03:31,333 --> 00:03:44,533
Teal cells sit inside one zone and are answered by cell
number. Amber cells cross a boundary and still need a test.

20
00:03:45,133 --> 00:04:00,233
Grids only come in fixed cell sizes, so these four do not match.
That makes this a picture of the shapes, not a fair comparison.

21
00:04:01,233 --> 00:04:12,733
This chart asks one question of every grid: how many
point-in-zone tests does it need? Lower is better.

22
00:04:13,333 --> 00:04:23,133
Each dot is one grid at one cell size.
Moving right means smaller cells.

23
00:04:23,733 --> 00:04:34,933
As cells shrink, every grid needs fewer tests,
and all four follow almost the same line.

24
00:04:35,533 --> 00:04:47,933
The dashed line is the R-tree. Grids with big cells sit
above it, which means more work than the R-tree.

25
00:04:48,533 --> 00:05:01,233
To compare fairly, read all four at the same cell size.
We use one city block, 0.02 square kilometers.

26
00:05:02,233 --> 00:05:13,933
Now all four grids have cells the size of one city block.
Their results are within about 8 percent of each other.

27
00:05:14,633 --> 00:05:28,933
One more check. Zone edges follow the street grid, so a grid
could get lucky by lining up with it. We turned the city six ways.

28
00:05:29,533 --> 00:05:42,433
The thin lines show how far each result moved. Square cells
swing by 15 to 19 percent. Hexagons and pentagons barely move.

29
00:05:42,933 --> 00:05:56,833
H3 needs the fewest tests because a hexagon has the least
edge for its area, so fewer cells cross a zone boundary.

30
00:05:58,397 --> 00:06:08,097
Brute force takes over five seconds. The R-tree
returns the same answer in under one.

31
00:06:08,523 --> 00:06:24,344
With cell IDs stored, a grid index finishes in about a fifth
of a second. The four grids differ by less than timing noise.

32
00:06:26,550 --> 00:06:39,020
On speed the grids are close.
Choose on what else you need from the index.

33
00:06:39,560 --> 00:06:53,640
Then tune the resolution and measure
on your own data.

34
00:06:55,683 --> 00:07:04,913
That was the spatial join. Thank you for watching.
